/* ============================================================
   PyTorch Q&A — single source of truth for every module page
   and the Q&A bank. m = module id, s = section (tab) id,
   imp = must-know. Answers are HTML; optional `code` renders
   as a highlighted, copyable block.
   ============================================================ */
window.PT_QA = [
  /* ---------------- 01 · Intro ---------------- */
  { m: 'intro', s: 'what', imp: true,
    q: 'What is PyTorch, and why is it so widely used?',
    a: '<p>PyTorch is an open-source deep learning library for Python. It gives you two things: <strong>tensors</strong> (n-dimensional arrays that run on CPU or GPU) and <strong>autograd</strong> (automatic differentiation that computes gradients for you). On top of that it ships building blocks for neural networks (<code>torch.nn</code>), optimizers (<code>torch.optim</code>) and data loading (<code>torch.utils.data</code>).</p><p>It became popular because it feels like ordinary Python: models are classes, the forward pass is a normal function, and you can use <code>print</code>, <code>if</code> and a debugger anywhere. That made it the default in research, and the same code now scales to production with <code>torch.compile</code>, ONNX export and distributed training.</p>' },
  { m: 'intro', s: 'what',
    q: 'Where did PyTorch come from?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>2002 — Torch</strong>: a scientific computing framework written in Lua. Fast, but Lua limited adoption.</li><li><strong>2016–17 — PyTorch</strong>: Facebook AI Research (now Meta AI) re-built Torch\'s tensor engine for Python and added dynamic computation graphs. Public release January 2017.</li><li><strong>2018 — PyTorch 1.0</strong>: merged with Caffe2 for production and introduced TorchScript.</li><li><strong>2022 — PyTorch Foundation</strong>: governance moved to the Linux Foundation.</li><li><strong>2023 — PyTorch 2.0</strong>: added <code>torch.compile</code>, which speeds up models without changing how you write them.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'intro', s: 'graphs', imp: true,
    q: 'What is the difference between a dynamic and a static computation graph?',
    a: '<p>A <strong>static graph</strong> (TensorFlow 1.x style) is declared first and executed later: you build the whole graph, compile it, then feed data through a session. It can be optimised ahead of time, but it is hard to debug and awkward for inputs whose structure changes.</p><p>A <strong>dynamic graph</strong> (PyTorch, "define-by-run") is recorded while your code runs. Each forward pass builds a fresh graph, so ordinary Python control flow — loops over a variable-length sentence, an <code>if</code> on a tensor value — just works, and errors point to the exact Python line.</p>' },
  { m: 'intro', s: 'graphs',
    q: 'If graphs are dynamic, how does PyTorch get compiler-level speed?',
    a: '<p>Since PyTorch 2.0, <code>torch.compile(model)</code> captures the graph from your Python code at run time (TorchDynamo), then fuses and optimises the operations (TorchInductor generates Triton/C++ kernels). You keep eager-mode development and get much of the benefit of a static graph. If your code does something the compiler can\'t capture, it falls back to eager for that part (a "graph break") instead of failing.</p>' },
  { m: 'intro', s: 'compare', imp: true,
    q: 'How does PyTorch compare with TensorFlow today?',
    a: '<p>The old split (PyTorch = dynamic, TensorFlow = static) is gone: TensorFlow 2 runs eagerly by default and PyTorch has <code>torch.compile</code>. The real differences now are:</p><ul><li><strong>Style:</strong> PyTorch is explicit — you write the training loop. TensorFlow usually goes through Keras <code>model.fit()</code>.</li><li><strong>Adoption:</strong> PyTorch dominates research papers and the Hugging Face ecosystem; TensorFlow still has a large installed base in older production systems.</li><li><strong>Deployment:</strong> TensorFlow has TF Lite / TF.js / TF Serving; PyTorch has ONNX export, ExecuTorch for edge devices and many serving options (Triton, vLLM, TorchServe).</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'intro', s: 'ecosystem',
    q: 'What are the core modules of PyTorch and what does each do?',
    a: '<ul><li><code>torch</code> — tensors and maths operations.</li><li><code>torch.autograd</code> — automatic differentiation.</li><li><code>torch.nn</code> — layers, losses and the <code>nn.Module</code> base class.</li><li><code>torch.nn.functional</code> — stateless versions of activations, losses, convolutions.</li><li><code>torch.optim</code> — optimizers (SGD, Adam, AdamW) and learning-rate schedulers.</li><li><code>torch.utils.data</code> — <code>Dataset</code>, <code>DataLoader</code>, samplers.</li><li><code>torch.cuda</code> / <code>torch.backends.mps</code> — GPU utilities.</li><li><code>torch.distributed</code> — multi-GPU and multi-node training (DDP, FSDP).</li><li><code>torch.compile</code>, <code>torch.export</code>, <code>torch.onnx</code> — speed and deployment.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'intro', s: 'ecosystem',
    q: 'Which libraries around PyTorch should you know?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Domain libraries:</strong> torchvision (images, pretrained CNNs, transforms), torchaudio (audio). torchtext is no longer actively developed — text work has moved to Hugging Face.</li><li><strong>Hugging Face Transformers / Datasets</strong> — pretrained language and vision models.</li><li><strong>PyTorch Lightning</strong> — removes training-loop boilerplate.</li><li><strong>torchmetrics</strong> — metrics that work across devices.</li><li><strong>Optuna</strong> — hyperparameter tuning.</li><li><strong>ONNX Runtime, TensorRT</strong> — fast inference.</li></ul>' },

  /* ---------------- 02 · Tensors ---------------- */
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'create', imp: true,
    q: 'What is a tensor, and how is it different from a NumPy array?',
    a: '<p>A tensor is an n-dimensional array: a scalar is 0-D, a vector 1-D, a matrix 2-D, a batch of RGB images 4-D <code>(N, C, H, W)</code>. A PyTorch tensor behaves almost exactly like a NumPy array, with two additions that make deep learning possible:</p><ul><li>it can live on a <strong>GPU</strong> (<code>.to("cuda")</code>) for massively parallel maths;</li><li>it can <strong>track gradients</strong> (<code>requires_grad=True</code>) so autograd can differentiate through it.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'create', imp: true,
    q: '<code>torch.tensor</code> vs <code>torch.Tensor</code> vs <code>torch.from_numpy</code> vs <code>torch.as_tensor</code>?',
    a: '<ul><li><code>torch.tensor(data)</code> — the recommended factory. Always <strong>copies</strong> the data and infers the dtype (ints → int64, floats → float32).</li><li><code>torch.Tensor(...)</code> — the class constructor. Always float32, and <code>torch.Tensor(3)</code> creates an <em>uninitialised</em> tensor of size 3, not the value 3. Avoid it.</li><li><code>torch.from_numpy(arr)</code> — <strong>shares memory</strong> with the NumPy array; changing one changes the other.</li><li><code>torch.as_tensor(data)</code> — shares memory when it can, copies when it must.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'create',
    q: 'Why would anyone use <code>torch.empty</code> instead of <code>torch.zeros</code>?',
    a: '<p><code>torch.empty</code> only allocates memory and skips writing values, so it is slightly faster — but it contains whatever garbage was in memory. Use it only when you will overwrite every element straight away (for example, a buffer you fill in a loop).</p>' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'create',
    q: 'What is the difference between <code>rand</code>, <code>randn</code> and <code>randint</code>, and between <code>arange</code> and <code>linspace</code>?',
    a: '<ul><li><code>rand</code> — uniform on [0, 1). <code>randn</code> — standard normal (mean 0, std 1). <code>randint(low, high, size)</code> — integers in [low, high).</li><li><code>arange(start, end, step)</code> — fixed <em>step</em>, end excluded. <code>linspace(start, end, steps)</code> — fixed <em>number of points</em>, end included.</li></ul><p>Call <code>torch.manual_seed(42)</code> first to make random tensors reproducible.</p>' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'dtypes',
    q: 'Why is float32 the default for deep learning, and when do you need other dtypes?',
    a: '<p>float32 is precise enough for training and is what GPUs are fastest at for general maths. You will meet others:</p><ul><li><code>int64</code> (<code>torch.long</code>) — class labels for <code>CrossEntropyLoss</code> and indices for <code>nn.Embedding</code>.</li><li><code>float16</code> / <code>bfloat16</code> — mixed-precision training to halve memory and speed up tensor cores.</li><li><code>bool</code> — masks.</li><li><code>float64</code> — rarely; NumPy defaults to it, so tensors made with <code>from_numpy</code> often need <code>.float()</code>.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'dtypes',
    q: 'What happens when you combine tensors of different dtypes?',
    a: '<p>PyTorch applies <strong>type promotion</strong>: the result takes the "larger" category. <code>int64 + float32 → float32</code>; <code>float32 + float64 → float64</code>. Mismatches still bite in specific places — matrix multiplication of float32 by float64, or passing float labels where a loss expects <code>long</code> — so convert explicitly with <code>.float()</code>, <code>.long()</code> or <code>.to(torch.float32)</code>.</p>' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'ops', imp: true,
    q: 'What does <code>dim</code> mean in <code>sum</code>, <code>mean</code>, <code>max</code> and friends?',
    a: '<p><code>dim</code> is the axis that gets <strong>collapsed</strong>. For a tensor of shape <code>(2, 3)</code>, <code>x.sum(dim=0)</code> adds down the rows and returns shape <code>(3,)</code> — one value per column. <code>x.sum(dim=1)</code> adds across columns and returns <code>(2,)</code> — one value per row.</p><p>Add <code>keepdim=True</code> to keep the collapsed axis with size 1, e.g. <code>(2, 1)</code>. That is what you want when the result will be broadcast back against the original tensor, as in normalisation.</p>',
    code: 'x = torch.tensor([[1., 2., 3.],\n                  [4., 5., 6.]])\nx.sum(dim=0)                # tensor([5., 7., 9.])   shape (3,)\nx.sum(dim=1)                # tensor([ 6., 15.])     shape (2,)\nx.mean(dim=1, keepdim=True) # shape (2, 1)\nx / x.sum(dim=1, keepdim=True)  # each row sums to 1' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'ops', imp: true,
    q: '<code>*</code> vs <code>@</code> vs <code>torch.mm</code> vs <code>torch.matmul</code> vs <code>torch.bmm</code>?',
    a: '<ul><li><code>a * b</code> — element-wise product (with broadcasting).</li><li><code>a @ b</code> = <code>torch.matmul</code> — matrix product; handles 1-D, 2-D and batched N-D inputs with broadcasting of batch dims.</li><li><code>torch.mm</code> — strictly 2-D × 2-D, no broadcasting.</li><li><code>torch.bmm</code> — strictly batched 3-D: <code>(B, n, m) @ (B, m, p) → (B, n, p)</code>.</li></ul><p>The rule for <code>(n, m) @ (m, p)</code>: inner sizes must match and the result is <code>(n, p)</code>. The classic error <em>"mat1 and mat2 shapes cannot be multiplied"</em> means they don\'t.</p>' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'ops',
    q: 'What is the difference between <code>max</code> and <code>argmax</code>?',
    a: '<p><code>x.max(dim=1)</code> returns a named tuple <code>(values, indices)</code>. <code>x.argmax(dim=1)</code> returns only the indices. For classification, <code>logits.argmax(dim=1)</code> gives the predicted class per sample — no softmax needed because softmax doesn\'t change which entry is largest.</p>' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'broadcast', imp: true,
    q: 'What are the broadcasting rules?',
    a: '<p>Line the shapes up from the <strong>right</strong>. Two dimensions are compatible if they are equal or one of them is 1 (a missing dimension counts as 1). Size-1 dimensions are virtually stretched — no memory is copied. If any pair is incompatible you get <em>"The size of tensor a must match the size of tensor b"</em>.</p><p>Examples: <code>(3, 1) + (1, 4) → (3, 4)</code>; <code>(64, 10) + (10,) → (64, 10)</code>; <code>(2, 3) + (2,)</code> fails because 3 ≠ 2.</p>' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'broadcast', imp: true,
    q: 'Why can broadcasting silently break your loss?',
    a: '<p>If predictions have shape <code>(N, 1)</code> and targets have shape <code>(N,)</code>, then <code>pred - y</code> broadcasts to <code>(N, N)</code>: every prediction is compared with every target. The loss is wrong but there is no error, and the model trains badly. <code>nn.MSELoss</code> warns about this; your own formulas won\'t. Fix it with <code>pred.squeeze(1)</code> or <code>y.unsqueeze(1)</code>, and check shapes with <code>assert pred.shape == y.shape</code>.</p>' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'index',
    q: 'How do boolean masks and <code>torch.where</code> work?',
    a: '<p>A comparison like <code>x &gt; 0</code> returns a bool tensor of the same shape. <code>x[mask]</code> selects the matching elements into a 1-D tensor; <code>x[mask] = 0</code> writes to them. <code>torch.where(cond, a, b)</code> picks from <code>a</code> where the condition holds and from <code>b</code> elsewhere, keeping the shape — the vectorised version of an if/else.</p>' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'index',
    q: 'Does slicing a tensor copy it?',
    a: '<p>Basic slicing (<code>x[1:, ::2]</code>, <code>x[:, 0]</code>) returns a <strong>view</strong> — same memory, different shape/strides — so writing to the slice changes the original. Indexing with a list, tensor of indices or boolean mask ("advanced indexing") returns a <strong>copy</strong>.</p>' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'reshape', imp: true,
    q: 'What is the difference between <code>view</code> and <code>reshape</code>?',
    a: '<p>Both change the shape without changing the data. <code>view</code> never copies: it requires the tensor to be <strong>contiguous</strong> in memory and raises an error otherwise. <code>reshape</code> returns a view when possible and silently copies when not. Use <code>reshape</code> unless you specifically need the guarantee of shared memory. Use <code>-1</code> to let PyTorch infer one dimension: <code>x.view(x.size(0), -1)</code>.</p>' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'reshape', imp: true,
    q: 'What does "contiguous" mean, and why does <code>transpose</code> break <code>view</code>?',
    a: '<p>A tensor is stored as one flat block of memory plus <strong>strides</strong> — how many elements to jump to move one step along each dimension. A freshly created <code>(2, 3)</code> tensor has strides <code>(3, 1)</code>: row-major, contiguous. <code>transpose</code>/<code>permute</code> don\'t move data; they just swap the strides to <code>(1, 3)</code>. The elements are no longer laid out in order, so <code>view</code> can\'t reinterpret them. Call <code>.contiguous()</code> (which copies into a fresh, ordered block) or use <code>reshape</code>.</p>' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'reshape',
    q: '<code>permute</code> vs <code>transpose</code>, and <code>squeeze</code> vs <code>unsqueeze</code>?',
    a: '<ul><li><code>transpose(d0, d1)</code> swaps exactly two dimensions; <code>permute(...)</code> reorders all of them — e.g. image <code>(H, W, C) → (C, H, W)</code> with <code>permute(2, 0, 1)</code>.</li><li><code>unsqueeze(d)</code> inserts a size-1 dimension (add a batch dim: <code>img.unsqueeze(0)</code>); <code>squeeze(d)</code> removes a size-1 dimension. Always pass <code>d</code> — a bare <code>squeeze()</code> on a batch of size 1 also removes the batch dimension.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'memory', imp: true,
    q: 'What are in-place operations, and why can they break autograd?',
    a: '<p>Methods ending in an underscore — <code>add_</code>, <code>relu_</code>, <code>zero_</code> — modify the tensor\'s memory directly instead of returning a new tensor. They save memory, but autograd often needs the original values to compute gradients. Overwriting them raises <em>"one of the variables needed for gradient computation has been modified by an inplace operation"</em>. Avoid in-place ops on tensors that are part of the graph; they are fine inside <code>torch.no_grad()</code> (which is exactly how optimizers update weights).</p>' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'memory', imp: true,
    q: '<code>b = a</code> vs <code>a.clone()</code> vs <code>a.detach()</code>?',
    a: '<ul><li><code>b = a</code> — no copy at all; both names point to the same tensor.</li><li><code>a.clone()</code> — new memory with the same values; stays connected to the autograd graph (gradients flow back to <code>a</code>).</li><li><code>a.detach()</code> — same memory, but cut out of the graph (no gradients).</li><li><code>a.detach().clone()</code> — an independent copy with no graph: the safe way to snapshot a value.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'memory',
    q: 'How do tensors and NumPy arrays share memory?',
    a: '<p><code>torch.from_numpy(arr)</code> and <code>t.numpy()</code> share the underlying buffer on CPU, so an in-place change to one shows up in the other. <code>t.numpy()</code> fails for tensors on a GPU (move them with <code>.cpu()</code> first) and for tensors that require grad (call <code>.detach()</code> first): <code>t.detach().cpu().numpy()</code>.</p>' },
  { m: 'tensors', s: 'memory',
    q: '<code>.item()</code> vs <code>.tolist()</code>?',
    a: '<p><code>.item()</code> converts a one-element tensor to a Python number — use it when logging the loss (<code>loss.item()</code>) so you don\'t keep the whole graph alive. <code>.tolist()</code> converts any tensor to nested Python lists. Both force a GPU→CPU sync, so avoid calling them on every step of a hot loop.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 03 · Autograd ---------------- */
  { m: 'autograd', s: 'why', imp: true,
    q: 'What is autograd and why do we need it?',
    a: '<p>Training needs the gradient of the loss with respect to every parameter. Deriving those by hand is fine for <code>y = x²</code> but impossible for a network with millions of weights. Autograd records every operation on tensors with <code>requires_grad=True</code> into a graph during the forward pass. Calling <code>loss.backward()</code> walks that graph in reverse, applies the chain rule at each node, and stores ∂loss/∂param in each leaf tensor\'s <code>.grad</code>.</p>' },
  { m: 'autograd', s: 'why',
    q: 'Walk through the chain rule for a single neuron.',
    a: '<p>For <code>z = w·x + b</code>, <code>ŷ = σ(z)</code> and binary cross-entropy loss <code>L</code>, the chain rule gives ∂L/∂w = ∂L/∂ŷ · ∂ŷ/∂z · ∂z/∂w. With BCE and a sigmoid these simplify neatly: ∂L/∂z = ŷ − y, so ∂L/∂w = (ŷ − y)·x and ∂L/∂b = ŷ − y. Autograd produces exactly these numbers — the Autograd lab lets you check them side by side.</p>' },
  { m: 'autograd', s: 'graph',
    q: 'What are leaf tensors, and why is <code>.grad</code> None for intermediate results?',
    a: '<p>A <strong>leaf</strong> is a tensor you created directly with <code>requires_grad=True</code> — in practice, model parameters. Intermediate results (<code>y = x ** 2</code>) are non-leaf: they have a <code>grad_fn</code> recording how they were made. To save memory, autograd only keeps <code>.grad</code> for leaves. If you want the gradient of an intermediate tensor, call <code>y.retain_grad()</code> before <code>backward()</code>.</p>' },
  { m: 'autograd', s: 'graph',
    q: 'What is <code>grad_fn</code>?',
    a: '<p>Every tensor produced by an operation on tracked tensors has a <code>grad_fn</code> — the backward function for that operation, e.g. <code>&lt;MulBackward0&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;SigmoidBackward0&gt;</code>. Following <code>grad_fn.next_functions</code> walks the graph back to the leaves. Leaves have <code>grad_fn = None</code>.</p>' },
  { m: 'autograd', s: 'graph',
    q: 'Why does <code>backward()</code> complain "grad can be implicitly created only for scalar outputs"?',
    a: '<p><code>backward()</code> with no arguments computes the derivative of a <strong>scalar</strong>. If the output is a vector, the gradient is a Jacobian and PyTorch needs to know how to weight each output. Either reduce first (<code>y.sum().backward()</code> or <code>loss.mean()</code>) or pass a tensor of weights: <code>y.backward(torch.ones_like(y))</code>.</p>' },
  { m: 'autograd', s: 'graph',
    q: 'What does <code>retain_graph=True</code> do?',
    a: '<p>After <code>backward()</code>, PyTorch frees the graph\'s saved intermediate values to save memory, so calling <code>backward()</code> a second time on the same graph fails. <code>retain_graph=True</code> keeps them. You rarely need it — usually only when two losses share part of a graph and you call <code>backward</code> on each separately (summing the losses and calling once is simpler).</p>' },
  { m: 'autograd', s: 'accumulate', imp: true,
    q: 'Why do gradients accumulate, and why must you call <code>zero_grad()</code>?',
    a: '<p><code>backward()</code> <strong>adds</strong> into <code>.grad</code> rather than overwriting it. That design lets you sum gradients from several backward passes. But in a normal training loop it means each step would use the sum of all previous gradients — so you reset them every iteration with <code>optimizer.zero_grad()</code> (or <code>x.grad.zero_()</code> for a raw tensor).</p>' },
  { m: 'autograd', s: 'accumulate',
    q: 'How do you use gradient accumulation to train with a bigger effective batch?',
    a: '<p>If a batch of 128 doesn\'t fit in GPU memory, run 4 batches of 32, call <code>backward()</code> on each (dividing the loss by 4), and only call <code>optimizer.step()</code> and <code>zero_grad()</code> every 4th batch. The summed gradients equal those of one batch of 128 (except for BatchNorm, which still sees 32 at a time).</p>',
    code: 'accum = 4\nfor i, (xb, yb) in enumerate(loader):\n    loss = loss_fn(model(xb), yb) / accum\n    loss.backward()                 # gradients add up\n    if (i + 1) % accum == 0:\n        optimizer.step()\n        optimizer.zero_grad()' },
  { m: 'autograd', s: 'control', imp: true,
    q: '<code>torch.no_grad()</code> vs <code>.detach()</code> vs <code>requires_grad_(False)</code>?',
    a: '<ul><li><code>with torch.no_grad():</code> — nothing inside the block is recorded. Use it for evaluation, inference and manual weight updates. Saves memory and time.</li><li><code>t.detach()</code> — returns a single tensor cut off from the graph; the rest of the computation is still tracked.</li><li><code>param.requires_grad_(False)</code> — permanently stops tracking that parameter. Used to <strong>freeze</strong> layers in transfer learning.</li></ul><p><code>torch.inference_mode()</code> is a stricter, slightly faster <code>no_grad</code> for pure inference.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 04 · Pipeline ---------------- */
  { m: 'pipeline', s: 'loop', imp: true,
    q: 'What are the steps of a PyTorch training loop?',
    a: '<ol><li><strong>Forward pass</strong> — <code>y_pred = model(x)</code></li><li><strong>Loss</strong> — <code>loss = loss_fn(y_pred, y)</code></li><li><strong>Clear old gradients</strong> — <code>optimizer.zero_grad()</code></li><li><strong>Backward pass</strong> — <code>loss.backward()</code></li><li><strong>Update</strong> — <code>optimizer.step()</code></li></ol><p>Repeat for every batch, for many epochs. <code>zero_grad</code> can come first or right before <code>backward</code>; what matters is that it happens once per step, before the new gradients are computed.</p>' },
  { m: 'pipeline', s: 'loop',
    q: 'What is the difference between an epoch, a batch and an iteration?',
    a: '<p>A <strong>batch</strong> is the group of samples processed in one forward/backward pass. An <strong>iteration</strong> (step) is one weight update — one batch. An <strong>epoch</strong> is one full pass over the training set. With 60,000 samples and batch size 64 there are ⌈60000 / 64⌉ = 938 iterations per epoch.</p>' },
  { m: 'pipeline', s: 'train',
    q: 'What happens if the learning rate is too high or too low?',
    a: '<p>Too <strong>low</strong>: the loss falls very slowly and training may stall before reaching a good solution. Too <strong>high</strong>: updates overshoot the minimum — the loss oscillates or explodes to <code>nan</code>. A good learning rate drops the loss quickly then settles. Try it in the live training lab.</p>' },
  { m: 'pipeline', s: 'scratch',
    q: 'Why is the manual weight update wrapped in <code>torch.no_grad()</code>?',
    a: '<p><code>w -= lr * w.grad</code> is itself an operation on a tensor that requires grad. Without <code>no_grad</code>, autograd would try to record the update into the graph (and in-place modification of a leaf raises an error). The update is bookkeeping, not part of the model, so it must not be tracked. Optimizers do the same thing internally.</p>' },
  { m: 'pipeline', s: 'scratch',
    q: 'Why scale or standardise input features before training?',
    a: '<p>When features have very different ranges (age 20–80, income 10⁴–10⁶), the loss surface becomes a long narrow valley and gradient descent zig-zags. Standardising each feature to mean 0, std 1 makes the surface rounder, so one learning rate works for all weights. Fit the scaler on the training set only, then apply it to validation and test data.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 05 · nn.Module ---------------- */
  { m: 'nn', s: 'module', imp: true,
    q: 'What is <code>nn.Module</code>, and what must a subclass implement?',
    a: '<p><code>nn.Module</code> is the base class for every layer and model. Subclass it, call <code>super().__init__()</code>, create layers as attributes in <code>__init__</code>, and implement <code>forward(self, x)</code>. Assigning layers as attributes registers their parameters automatically, which is what makes <code>model.parameters()</code>, <code>model.to(device)</code>, <code>model.train()</code>/<code>eval()</code> and <code>state_dict()</code> work.</p>' },
  { m: 'nn', s: 'module',
    q: 'Why call <code>model(x)</code> rather than <code>model.forward(x)</code>?',
    a: '<p><code>model(x)</code> goes through <code>nn.Module.__call__</code>, which runs registered hooks (used by profilers, quantisation, some debugging tools) and then calls <code>forward</code>. Calling <code>forward</code> directly skips the hooks and can give subtly different behaviour.</p>' },
  { m: 'nn', s: 'module', imp: true,
    q: 'Why use <code>nn.ModuleList</code> instead of a Python list of layers?',
    a: '<p>Parameters are only registered for attributes that are Modules. Layers stored in a plain Python <code>list</code> are invisible to <code>model.parameters()</code>, so the optimizer never updates them and <code>model.to("cuda")</code> doesn\'t move them — a silent bug. <code>nn.ModuleList</code> (and <code>nn.ModuleDict</code>) register them properly.</p>' },
  { m: 'nn', s: 'module',
    q: '<code>nn.Sequential</code> or a custom <code>nn.Module</code>?',
    a: '<p><code>nn.Sequential</code> is perfect when data flows straight through a chain of layers. Write a custom Module when you need anything else: skip connections, multiple inputs or outputs, branching, or logic in <code>forward</code>. You can mix them — a custom Module that contains Sequential blocks.</p>' },
  { m: 'nn', s: 'module',
    q: '<code>nn.ReLU()</code> or <code>F.relu()</code>?',
    a: '<p><code>torch.nn</code> layers are objects that may hold parameters and state; <code>torch.nn.functional</code> provides the same operations as plain functions. Layers with weights (Linear, Conv2d) or with train/eval behaviour (Dropout, BatchNorm) should be <code>nn</code> modules. Stateless activations can be either; <code>nn.ReLU()</code> is needed inside <code>nn.Sequential</code>.</p>' },
  { m: 'nn', s: 'module',
    q: 'How do you count a model\'s parameters?',
    a: '<p>Sum the number of elements of every parameter tensor. A <code>nn.Linear(in, out)</code> has <code>in × out</code> weights plus <code>out</code> biases.</p>',
    code: 'total = sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters())\ntrainable = sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters() if p.requires_grad)\nfor name, p in model.named_parameters():\n    print(name, tuple(p.shape))' },
  { m: 'nn', s: 'loss', imp: true,
    q: 'Which loss function goes with which task?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Regression:</strong> <code>nn.MSELoss</code> (penalises big errors), <code>nn.L1Loss</code> (robust to outliers), <code>nn.SmoothL1Loss</code>/<code>HuberLoss</code> (in between).</li><li><strong>Binary / multi-label:</strong> <code>nn.BCEWithLogitsLoss</code> — one raw logit per label, float targets 0/1.</li><li><strong>Multi-class (one correct class):</strong> <code>nn.CrossEntropyLoss</code> — raw logits of shape <code>(N, C)</code>, integer targets of shape <code>(N,)</code>.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'nn', s: 'loss', imp: true,
    q: 'Should you apply softmax before <code>nn.CrossEntropyLoss</code>?',
    a: '<p>No. <code>CrossEntropyLoss</code> already combines <code>LogSoftmax</code> and <code>NLLLoss</code>. Feeding it probabilities applies softmax twice, which squashes the gradients and slows learning. The model\'s last layer should output raw <strong>logits</strong>; apply <code>softmax</code> only when you need probabilities for display, and <code>argmax</code> for predictions.</p>' },
  { m: 'nn', s: 'loss', imp: true,
    q: '<code>BCELoss</code> vs <code>BCEWithLogitsLoss</code>?',
    a: '<p><code>BCELoss</code> expects probabilities, so you add a sigmoid yourself. <code>BCEWithLogitsLoss</code> takes logits and fuses sigmoid + BCE using the log-sum-exp trick, which is numerically stable: it doesn\'t produce <code>log(0) = -inf</code> when the sigmoid saturates. Prefer <code>BCEWithLogitsLoss</code>. Its <code>pos_weight</code> argument also helps with class imbalance.</p>' },
  { m: 'nn', s: 'optim', imp: true,
    q: 'SGD vs SGD with momentum vs Adam vs AdamW?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>SGD</strong> — step against the gradient: <code>w ← w − lr·g</code>. Simple; sensitive to the learning rate.</li><li><strong>Momentum</strong> — keeps a running velocity, so it speeds through flat regions and damps zig-zags.</li><li><strong>Adam</strong> — momentum plus a per-parameter learning rate scaled by the running average of squared gradients. Works well with little tuning; the usual default (<code>lr=1e-3</code>).</li><li><strong>AdamW</strong> — Adam with weight decay applied directly to the weights rather than mixed into the gradient. The standard choice for transformers and generally preferred when you use weight decay.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'nn', s: 'optim',
    q: 'What do <code>optimizer.step()</code> and a learning-rate scheduler do?',
    a: '<p>The optimizer holds references to the parameters you passed in. <code>step()</code> reads each parameter\'s <code>.grad</code> and updates the parameter in place according to its rule. A scheduler (<code>StepLR</code>, <code>CosineAnnealingLR</code>, <code>OneCycleLR</code>, <code>ReduceLROnPlateau</code>) changes the optimizer\'s learning rate over time; call <code>scheduler.step()</code> after <code>optimizer.step()</code> — usually once per epoch, or once per batch for OneCycle.</p>' },

  { m: 'nn', s: 'refactor',
    q: 'Why is <code>logits &gt; 0</code> the same as <code>sigmoid(logits) &gt; 0.5</code>?',
    a: '<p>The sigmoid is monotonic and σ(0) = 0.5, so a logit above zero always maps to a probability above one half. Thresholding the logit directly skips a needless sigmoid at inference. For a different threshold t, compare against <code>log(t / (1 − t))</code>.</p>' },
  { m: 'nn', s: 'refactor',
    q: 'What does <code>model.parameters()</code> return, and why pass it to the optimizer?',
    a: '<p>A generator over every registered parameter tensor (weights and biases of all sub-modules). The optimizer keeps references to those exact tensors; <code>step()</code> updates them in place using their <code>.grad</code>. That is why parameters must be registered (attributes or ModuleList) and why you should create the optimizer after building the model.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 06 · Data ---------------- */
  { m: 'data', s: 'why',
    q: 'Why train on mini-batches instead of the whole dataset at once?',
    a: '<p>The full dataset rarely fits in GPU memory, and one update per epoch is slow to converge. Single-sample updates are noisy and waste the GPU\'s parallelism. Mini-batches (typically 32–256) balance the two: many updates per epoch, efficient matrix maths, and a little gradient noise that often helps generalisation.</p>' },
  { m: 'data', s: 'dataset', imp: true,
    q: 'What must a custom <code>Dataset</code> implement?',
    a: '<p>A map-style dataset subclasses <code>torch.utils.data.Dataset</code> and implements <code>__len__</code> (number of samples) and <code>__getitem__(idx)</code> (return one sample, typically <code>(features, label)</code>). Load file paths or a dataframe in <code>__init__</code>, and do per-sample work — reading an image, applying transforms — in <code>__getitem__</code>, so it runs lazily and in parallel workers.</p>' },
  { m: 'data', s: 'dataset',
    q: 'What is an <code>IterableDataset</code>?',
    a: '<p>Instead of random access by index, it implements <code>__iter__</code> and yields samples one after another. Use it for streams that don\'t fit in memory or have no known length — log streams, huge sharded text corpora, database cursors. Shuffling and splitting across workers become your responsibility.</p>' },
  { m: 'data', s: 'loader',
    q: 'What are the most important <code>DataLoader</code> arguments?',
    a: '<ul><li><code>batch_size</code> — samples per batch.</li><li><code>shuffle=True</code> for training (new order each epoch), <code>False</code> for validation/test.</li><li><code>num_workers</code> — background processes that load data in parallel.</li><li><code>pin_memory=True</code> — page-locked memory for faster CPU→GPU copies.</li><li><code>drop_last</code> — drop the final incomplete batch.</li><li><code>collate_fn</code> — how a list of samples is merged into a batch.</li><li><code>sampler</code> — custom sampling order (mutually exclusive with <code>shuffle</code>).</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'data', s: 'loader',
    q: 'Why shuffle the training set but not the validation set?',
    a: '<p>Shuffling every epoch stops the model from learning the order of the data and decorrelates consecutive batches (imagine a dataset sorted by class: every batch would contain a single class). Validation metrics don\'t depend on order, so shuffling there only makes debugging harder.</p>' },
  { m: 'data', s: 'collate',
    q: 'What is <code>collate_fn</code>, and when do you need a custom one?',
    a: '<p>The DataLoader fetches <code>batch_size</code> samples, then calls <code>collate_fn(list_of_samples)</code> to merge them. The default stacks tensors, which requires every sample to have the same shape. For variable-length data — sentences, audio clips — write a <code>collate_fn</code> that pads each batch to its longest item (<code>nn.utils.rnn.pad_sequence</code>) and returns the lengths too.</p>',
    code: 'from torch.nn.utils.rnn import pad_sequence\n\ndef collate(batch):\n    seqs, labels = zip(*batch)\n    lengths = torch.tensor([len(s) for s in seqs])\n    padded = pad_sequence(seqs, batch_first=True, padding_value=0)\n    return padded, torch.tensor(labels), lengths\n\nloader = DataLoader(ds, batch_size=32, shuffle=True, collate_fn=collate)' },
  { m: 'data', s: 'sampler',
    q: 'How do you handle class imbalance with a sampler?',
    a: '<p><code>WeightedRandomSampler</code> draws samples with probability proportional to a weight, so giving each sample the weight <code>1 / count(its class)</code> makes batches roughly balanced. Pass it as <code>sampler=</code> and drop <code>shuffle=True</code>. Alternatives: class weights in the loss (<code>CrossEntropyLoss(weight=...)</code>, <code>BCEWithLogitsLoss(pos_weight=...)</code>).</p>' },
  { m: 'data', s: 'sampler',
    q: 'How do you pick <code>num_workers</code>?',
    a: '<p>If the GPU sits idle waiting for data, loading is the bottleneck. Start with 2–4 workers per GPU (up to the number of CPU cores) and increase while throughput improves. Use <code>persistent_workers=True</code> to avoid restarting workers every epoch. On Windows/macOS, worker code must sit under <code>if __name__ == "__main__":</code>. In notebooks, <code>num_workers=0</code> is the safe default.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 07 · ANN & GPU ---------------- */
  { m: 'ann', s: 'arch',
    q: 'How many parameters does a <code>784 → 128 → 64 → 10</code> network have?',
    a: '<p>Each <code>Linear(in, out)</code> has <code>in·out + out</code> parameters: 784·128 + 128 = 100,480; 128·64 + 64 = 8,256; 64·10 + 10 = 650. Total: <strong>109,386</strong>. Activations, Dropout and Flatten have none; BatchNorm1d adds 2 per feature.</p>' },
  { m: 'ann', s: 'arch',
    q: 'Why is there no softmax at the end of the classifier?',
    a: '<p>Because <code>nn.CrossEntropyLoss</code> applies log-softmax internally. The network outputs 10 logits; <code>argmax</code> gives the predicted class, and <code>softmax</code> is applied only if you need probabilities.</p>' },
  { m: 'ann', s: 'train', imp: true,
    q: 'What do <code>model.train()</code> and <code>model.eval()</code> change?',
    a: '<p>They switch layers whose behaviour differs between training and inference. <strong>Dropout</strong> drops units in train mode and does nothing in eval. <strong>BatchNorm</strong> uses the current batch\'s statistics in train mode (and updates its running averages) and uses the stored running averages in eval. Forgetting <code>model.eval()</code> before validation gives noisy, wrong metrics; forgetting <code>model.train()</code> afterwards silently disables dropout.</p>' },
  { m: 'ann', s: 'train', imp: true,
    q: 'Is <code>model.eval()</code> the same as <code>torch.no_grad()</code>?',
    a: '<p>No — they do different jobs and you normally use both for evaluation. <code>model.eval()</code> changes layer behaviour (dropout, batch norm) but still records gradients. <code>torch.no_grad()</code> stops gradient recording to save memory and time but doesn\'t change layers.</p>',
    code: 'model.eval()\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    for xb, yb in val_loader:\n        logits = model(xb.to(device))\n        correct += (logits.argmax(1) == yb.to(device)).sum().item()\nmodel.train()' },
  { m: 'ann', s: 'gpu', imp: true,
    q: 'How do you train on a GPU?',
    a: '<p>Pick a device once, move the model there once, and move every batch there inside the loop. Create the optimizer after moving the model (or at least never re-create the parameters).</p>',
    code: 'device = "cuda" if torch.cuda.is_available() else (\n         "mps" if torch.backends.mps.is_available() else "cpu")\nmodel = MyNet().to(device)\nfor xb, yb in loader:\n    xb, yb = xb.to(device, non_blocking=True), yb.to(device, non_blocking=True)\n    ...' },
  { m: 'ann', s: 'gpu',
    q: 'What causes "Expected all tensors to be on the same device"?',
    a: '<p>An operation received tensors on different devices — usually the model is on <code>cuda</code> but the batch, the labels, or a tensor created inside <code>forward</code> (like <code>torch.zeros(...)</code>) is on the CPU. Move inputs and labels with <code>.to(device)</code>, and create new tensors with <code>device=x.device</code> (or register them as buffers so <code>model.to()</code> moves them).</p>' },
  { m: 'ann', s: 'gpu',
    q: 'Why is <code>model.to(device)</code> enough but <code>x.to(device)</code> must be reassigned?',
    a: '<p><code>nn.Module.to()</code> moves the module\'s parameters in place and returns the module itself, so <code>model.to(device)</code> works on its own. <code>Tensor.to()</code> returns a <strong>new</strong> tensor and leaves the original where it was, so you must write <code>x = x.to(device)</code>.</p>' },
  { m: 'ann', s: 'gpu',
    q: 'Why might a GPU be slower than a CPU for a small model?',
    a: '<p>Launching GPU kernels and copying data across PCIe has fixed overhead. With a tiny network and small batches the GPU sits mostly idle. GPUs win when there is enough parallel work: larger batches, convolutions, big matrix multiplies. Other common slow-downs: calling <code>.item()</code> every step (forces a sync) and data loading with <code>num_workers=0</code>.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 08 · Optimizing ---------------- */
  { m: 'optimize', s: 'overfit', imp: true,
    q: 'How do you recognise overfitting, and what are the main fixes?',
    a: '<p>Training loss keeps falling while validation loss stops improving and starts rising — the model is memorising the training set. Fixes, roughly in order: get more data or use augmentation; add dropout; add weight decay; use batch norm; make the model smaller; stop early at the best validation epoch.</p>' },
  { m: 'optimize', s: 'overfit',
    q: 'How does early stopping work?',
    a: '<p>After each epoch, evaluate on the validation set. Keep a copy of the weights with the best validation score. If the score hasn\'t improved for <code>patience</code> epochs, stop and restore the best weights.</p>' },
  { m: 'optimize', s: 'dropout', imp: true,
    q: 'How does dropout work, and why are surviving activations scaled up?',
    a: '<p>During training, <code>nn.Dropout(p)</code> zeroes each activation with probability <code>p</code>, so the network can\'t rely on any single unit and effectively trains an ensemble of thinned networks. PyTorch uses <strong>inverted dropout</strong>: survivors are multiplied by <code>1/(1−p)</code> so the expected activation stays the same. That is why nothing needs rescaling at inference — in <code>eval()</code> mode dropout is simply the identity.</p>' },
  { m: 'optimize', s: 'dropout',
    q: 'Where should dropout go, and what value of p?',
    a: '<p>Typically after the activation of hidden fully-connected layers, with <code>p</code> = 0.2–0.5. Not on the output layer. In CNNs it is used mostly in the classifier head; convolutional layers usually rely on batch norm and augmentation instead.</p>' },
  { m: 'optimize', s: 'batchnorm', imp: true,
    q: 'What does batch normalisation do, and how does it differ between train and eval?',
    a: '<p>For each feature, BatchNorm subtracts the batch mean and divides by the batch standard deviation, then applies a learned scale γ and shift β. That keeps activations in a stable range, allows higher learning rates and speeds up training. In <strong>train</strong> mode it uses the current batch\'s statistics and updates running averages; in <strong>eval</strong> mode it uses those running averages, so predictions don\'t depend on what else is in the batch.</p>' },
  { m: 'optimize', s: 'batchnorm',
    q: 'Why does BatchNorm fail with a batch size of 1, and what is LayerNorm?',
    a: '<p>With one sample per batch the variance of each feature is zero, so training-mode BatchNorm has nothing to normalise (PyTorch raises an error for <code>BatchNorm1d</code>). Very small batches give noisy statistics too. <strong>LayerNorm</strong> normalises across the features of each sample instead of across the batch, so it doesn\'t depend on batch size — which is why transformers and RNNs use it.</p>' },
  { m: 'optimize', s: 'weightdecay', imp: true,
    q: 'What is weight decay, and why use AdamW instead of Adam with <code>weight_decay</code>?',
    a: '<p>Weight decay pulls every weight towards zero a little at each step, penalising large weights (equivalent to L2 regularisation for plain SGD). In Adam, adding <code>weight_decay</code> mixes the penalty into the gradient, where Adam\'s adaptive scaling weakens it unevenly. <strong>AdamW</strong> decouples it and shrinks the weights directly, which regularises as intended. Typical values: 1e-4 to 1e-2.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 09 · Optuna ---------------- */
  { m: 'optuna', s: 'what',
    q: 'What is the difference between parameters and hyperparameters?',
    a: '<p><strong>Parameters</strong> (weights, biases) are learned by gradient descent. <strong>Hyperparameters</strong> are chosen before training and control how learning happens: learning rate, batch size, number and size of layers, dropout rate, weight decay, optimizer, epochs. Tuning means searching for the hyperparameters that give the best <em>validation</em> score.</p>' },
  { m: 'optuna', s: 'search', imp: true,
    q: 'Grid search vs random search vs Bayesian optimisation (TPE)?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Grid</strong> tries every combination. The cost grows exponentially with the number of hyperparameters, and it tests only a few distinct values of each.</li><li><strong>Random</strong> samples combinations. With the same budget it tries many more distinct values of the hyperparameters that matter most, so it usually beats grid.</li><li><strong>Bayesian / TPE</strong> (Optuna\'s default) learns from finished trials which regions score well and samples more there. It reaches good results in fewer trials.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'optuna', s: 'code',
    q: 'How do you tune a PyTorch model with Optuna?',
    a: '<p>Write an <code>objective(trial)</code> that samples hyperparameters with <code>trial.suggest_*</code>, builds and trains the model, and returns the validation metric. Create a study with the right <code>direction</code> and call <code>optimize</code>.</p>',
    code: 'import optuna\n\ndef objective(trial):\n    lr = trial.suggest_float("lr", 1e-5, 1e-1, log=True)\n    hidden = trial.suggest_int("hidden", 32, 256, step=32)\n    dropout = trial.suggest_float("dropout", 0.0, 0.5)\n    opt_name = trial.suggest_categorical("optimizer", ["Adam", "SGD"])\n    model = build_model(hidden, dropout).to(device)\n    opt = getattr(torch.optim, opt_name)(model.parameters(), lr=lr)\n    for epoch in range(10):\n        train_one_epoch(model, opt)\n        acc = evaluate(model)\n        trial.report(acc, epoch)\n        if trial.should_prune():\n            raise optuna.TrialPruned()\n    return acc\n\nstudy = optuna.create_study(direction="maximize")\nstudy.optimize(objective, n_trials=50)\nprint(study.best_params)' },
  { m: 'optuna', s: 'code',
    q: 'What is pruning in Optuna?',
    a: '<p>A trial reports intermediate scores (for example, validation accuracy after each epoch) with <code>trial.report</code>. A pruner such as <code>MedianPruner</code> compares them with earlier trials at the same epoch and stops trials that are clearly worse. Bad configurations are abandoned early, so the same compute budget explores more of the space.</p>' },
  { m: 'optuna', s: 'code',
    q: 'Why sample the learning rate on a log scale?',
    a: '<p>The useful range spans orders of magnitude (1e-5 to 1e-1). Sampling uniformly would put about 90% of trials between 0.01 and 0.1. <code>log=True</code> samples evenly across each power of ten.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 10 · CNN ---------------- */
  { m: 'cnn', s: 'conv', imp: true,
    q: 'Why use CNNs for images instead of fully connected networks?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Parameter sharing:</strong> one 3×3 filter is reused across the whole image — 9 weights instead of one weight per pixel per neuron.</li><li><strong>Local connectivity:</strong> each output looks at a small neighbourhood, matching how visual features (edges, textures) are local.</li><li><strong>Translation equivariance:</strong> a cat in the corner activates the same filter as a cat in the centre.</li><li>Flattening a 224×224×3 image into a Linear layer of 1,000 units would need 150 million weights for one layer.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'cnn', s: 'conv', imp: true,
    q: 'What is the output-size formula for a convolution?',
    a: '<p>For input size <code>N</code>, kernel <code>K</code>, padding <code>P</code> and stride <code>S</code>: <strong>out = ⌊(N − K + 2P) / S⌋ + 1</strong>. With <code>K=3, P=1, S=1</code> the size is preserved ("same" padding). A 2×2 max-pool with stride 2 halves it. The same formula applies to pooling with P=0.</p>' },
  { m: 'cnn', s: 'conv',
    q: 'How many parameters does <code>nn.Conv2d(in_c, out_c, k)</code> have?',
    a: '<p>Each output channel has one <code>k × k</code> kernel per input channel plus one bias: <strong>out_c × (in_c × k × k + 1)</strong>. <code>Conv2d(3, 32, 3)</code> → 32 × (3·9 + 1) = 896. The count doesn\'t depend on image size.</p>' },
  { m: 'cnn', s: 'conv',
    q: 'What shape does <code>nn.Conv2d</code> expect?',
    a: '<p><code>(N, C, H, W)</code> — batch, channels, height, width ("channels first"). Images loaded with PIL/NumPy are <code>(H, W, C)</code>; <code>transforms.ToTensor()</code> converts to <code>(C, H, W)</code> and scales to [0, 1]. A single image needs a batch dim: <code>img.unsqueeze(0)</code>.</p>' },
  { m: 'cnn', s: 'pool',
    q: 'Max pooling vs average pooling — and what does <code>AdaptiveAvgPool2d</code> do?',
    a: '<p>Max pooling keeps the strongest activation in each window (is a feature present here?); average pooling keeps the mean. Both shrink the feature map and add a little translation invariance, with no parameters. <code>AdaptiveAvgPool2d((1, 1))</code> pools whatever size arrives down to a fixed output, so the classifier works for any input resolution — modern CNNs (ResNet) use it instead of a large flatten.</p>' },
  { m: 'cnn', s: 'shapes',
    q: 'How do you find the input size of the first Linear layer after the convolutions?',
    a: '<p>Apply the output-size formula layer by layer (the shape calculator does this for you), or let PyTorch work it out: pass a dummy tensor through the convolutional part and read the shape. <code>nn.LazyLinear(out)</code> infers its input size on the first forward pass.</p>',
    code: 'with torch.no_grad():\n    n = features(torch.zeros(1, 1, 28, 28)).flatten(1).shape[1]\nclassifier = nn.Linear(n, 10)\n# or simply: nn.LazyLinear(10)' },

  { m: 'cnn', s: 'code',
    q: 'What is data augmentation, and why apply it only to the training set?',
    a: '<p>Augmentation applies random, label-preserving changes — flips, small rotations, crops, colour jitter — each time an image is loaded, so the model sees a slightly different version every epoch. It is a cheap way to get "more data" and reduce overfitting. Validation and test sets must stay deterministic (only resize + normalise) so their scores are comparable and honest.</p>' },
  { m: 'cnn', s: 'code',
    q: 'Why do CNNs grow the number of channels while shrinking height and width?',
    a: '<p>Early layers detect simple, local patterns (edges) that need few filters but full resolution. Deeper layers combine them into more abstract parts (eyes, wheels) — many more kinds of feature, but where exactly they occur matters less. Pooling halves the resolution, which roughly halves compute per channel, so doubling the channels keeps the cost per layer balanced while each unit\'s receptive field grows.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 11 · Transfer ---------------- */
  { m: 'transfer', s: 'why', imp: true,
    q: 'What is transfer learning and why does it work?',
    a: '<p>Instead of training from random weights, start from a network pretrained on a large dataset such as ImageNet. Its early layers have learnt general features — edges, colours, textures — and its later layers more task-specific ones. Reusing them gives good accuracy with far less data and compute. You replace the final classification layer to match your classes and train that (and optionally some deeper layers) on your data.</p>' },
  { m: 'transfer', s: 'freeze', imp: true,
    q: 'Feature extraction vs fine-tuning?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Feature extraction:</strong> freeze the whole pretrained backbone (<code>requires_grad=False</code>) and train only the new head. Fast, works with little data.</li><li><strong>Fine-tuning:</strong> also unfreeze some (or all) of the backbone and train it with a small learning rate. Better when you have more data or your images differ from ImageNet.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'transfer', s: 'freeze',
    q: 'How do you freeze layers in PyTorch?',
    a: '<p>Set <code>requires_grad = False</code> on their parameters, then replace the head (new layers are trainable by default). Pass only trainable parameters to the optimizer. Frozen BatchNorm layers still update running statistics in train mode — call <code>.eval()</code> on them if you want them fully frozen.</p>',
    code: 'from torchvision.models import resnet18, ResNet18_Weights\n\nmodel = resnet18(weights=ResNet18_Weights.DEFAULT)\nfor p in model.parameters():\n    p.requires_grad = False\nmodel.fc = nn.Linear(model.fc.in_features, num_classes)  # trainable\nopt = torch.optim.Adam((p for p in model.parameters() if p.requires_grad), lr=1e-3)' },
  { m: 'transfer', s: 'strategy',
    q: 'How do dataset size and similarity decide the strategy?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Small, similar to ImageNet:</strong> feature extraction — train only the head.</li><li><strong>Large, similar:</strong> fine-tune the later blocks or the whole network.</li><li><strong>Small, different</strong> (X-rays, satellite images): train the head plus the last block or two; early generic features still help.</li><li><strong>Large, different:</strong> fine-tune everything, or even train from scratch.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'transfer', s: 'code',
    q: 'Why must you use the same preprocessing as the pretrained model?',
    a: '<p>The weights were learnt on images resized and normalised in a specific way (ImageNet mean <code>[0.485, 0.456, 0.406]</code>, std <code>[0.229, 0.224, 0.225]</code>, usually 224×224). Different preprocessing shifts the input distribution and degrades the features. torchvision bundles the right transforms with the weights: <code>ResNet18_Weights.DEFAULT.transforms()</code>.</p>' },
  { m: 'transfer', s: 'code',
    q: 'Why use a lower learning rate for pretrained layers?',
    a: '<p>Pretrained weights are already good; large updates would destroy what they learnt ("catastrophic forgetting"), especially while the randomly-initialised head is still producing large gradients. Use parameter groups — e.g. 1e-4 for the backbone and 1e-3 for the head.</p>',
    code: 'opt = torch.optim.AdamW([\n    {"params": model.layer4.parameters(), "lr": 1e-4},\n    {"params": model.fc.parameters(),     "lr": 1e-3},\n], weight_decay=1e-4)' },
  { m: 'transfer', s: 'code',
    q: '<code>pretrained=True</code> or <code>weights=</code>?',
    a: '<p>torchvision replaced <code>pretrained=True</code> with explicit weight enums: <code>resnet50(weights=ResNet50_Weights.IMAGENET1K_V2)</code> or <code>weights="DEFAULT"</code> for the best available. The enum also carries the matching transforms and class names. <code>pretrained=</code> has been deprecated since torchvision 0.13.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 12 · RNN ---------------- */
  { m: 'rnn', s: 'text',
    q: 'How is text turned into something a network can process?',
    a: '<ol><li><strong>Tokenise</strong> — split into words or sub-words.</li><li><strong>Build a vocabulary</strong> — map each token to an integer id, with special tokens such as <code>&lt;PAD&gt;</code> = 0 and <code>&lt;UNK&gt;</code> for words not seen in training.</li><li><strong>Numericalise</strong> — replace tokens with ids.</li><li><strong>Pad</strong> sequences in a batch to equal length.</li><li><strong>Embed</strong> — <code>nn.Embedding</code> turns each id into a dense vector.</li></ol>' },
  { m: 'rnn', s: 'embed', imp: true,
    q: 'What is <code>nn.Embedding</code>, and why not use one-hot vectors?',
    a: '<p><code>nn.Embedding(vocab_size, dim)</code> is a lookup table: row <code>i</code> is the learned vector for token <code>i</code>. It is mathematically the same as multiplying a one-hot vector by a weight matrix, but without building huge sparse one-hot tensors. One-hot vectors are all equally far apart; learned embeddings place similar words close together. Input must be <code>long</code> ids; output shape is <code>(*input_shape, dim)</code>. <code>padding_idx=0</code> keeps the padding vector at zero and untrained.</p>' },
  { m: 'rnn', s: 'unroll', imp: true,
    q: 'How does an RNN process a sequence?',
    a: '<p>It reads one token at a time and keeps a hidden state that summarises everything so far: <strong>hₜ = tanh(W<sub>x</sub>·xₜ + W<sub>h</sub>·hₜ₋₁ + b)</strong>. The <em>same</em> weights are reused at every time step, so the model handles any sequence length. The final hidden state (or all hidden states) feeds a Linear layer for the prediction.</p>' },
  { m: 'rnn', s: 'unroll',
    q: 'What shapes do <code>nn.RNN</code> / <code>nn.LSTM</code> take and return?',
    a: '<p>With <code>batch_first=True</code>, input is <code>(N, L, input_size)</code>. They return <code>output</code> of shape <code>(N, L, D·hidden)</code> — the hidden state at every step — and <code>h_n</code> of shape <code>(D·num_layers, N, hidden)</code> — the final state of each layer (D = 2 if bidirectional). Note that <code>h_n</code> is <strong>not</strong> batch-first even when the input is. LSTM returns <code>output, (h_n, c_n)</code>. For classification use <code>h_n[-1]</code> or <code>output[:, -1, :]</code>.</p>' },
  { m: 'rnn', s: 'qa',
    q: 'What are the limits of a simple RNN?',
    a: '<p>Gradients shrink (or explode) as they flow back through many time steps, so plain RNNs struggle to learn dependencies more than ~10–20 steps back. Processing is sequential, so it can\'t be parallelised across time like a transformer. LSTMs and GRUs fix the first problem; attention and transformers fix both.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 13 · LSTM ---------------- */
  { m: 'lstm', s: 'vanish', imp: true,
    q: 'What is the vanishing gradient problem in RNNs?',
    a: '<p>Back-propagating through time multiplies the gradient by the recurrent Jacobian at every step. If those factors are mostly below 1 (tanh saturates, small weights), the gradient shrinks exponentially — after 30 steps, 0.9³⁰ ≈ 0.04 — so early tokens barely influence learning. If they are above 1 it explodes instead. The LSTM\'s additive cell-state path lets gradients flow across many steps.</p>' },
  { m: 'lstm', s: 'vanish',
    q: 'What is gradient clipping?',
    a: '<p>It rescales the gradients when their overall norm exceeds a threshold, preventing one exploding step from wrecking the weights. Call it between <code>backward()</code> and <code>step()</code>: <code>torch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_(model.parameters(), max_norm=1.0)</code>. Standard for RNNs, LSTMs and transformers.</p>' },
  { m: 'lstm', s: 'gates', imp: true,
    q: 'Explain the LSTM gates.',
    a: '<p>An LSTM keeps a <strong>cell state</strong> cₜ (long-term memory) alongside the hidden state hₜ. Three sigmoid gates (values 0–1) control it:</p><ul><li><strong>Forget gate</strong> fₜ — how much of the old cell state to keep.</li><li><strong>Input gate</strong> iₜ — how much of the new candidate g̃ₜ = tanh(…) to write.</li><li><strong>Output gate</strong> oₜ — how much of the cell to expose as the hidden state.</li></ul><p>cₜ = fₜ ⊙ cₜ₋₁ + iₜ ⊙ g̃ₜ and hₜ = oₜ ⊙ tanh(cₜ). Because the cell update is additive, gradients can pass through many steps when fₜ ≈ 1.</p>' },
  { m: 'lstm', s: 'gates',
    q: 'LSTM vs GRU?',
    a: '<p>A GRU merges the forget and input gates into one <em>update</em> gate and has no separate cell state, so it has about 25% fewer parameters and trains faster. Accuracy is usually similar; LSTMs can be slightly better on long, complex sequences. In PyTorch the API is the same except LSTM returns <code>(h_n, c_n)</code> and GRU returns only <code>h_n</code>.</p>' },
  { m: 'lstm', s: 'data',
    q: 'How do you build training data for a next-word predictor?',
    a: '<p>Turn each sentence into all of its prefixes: "I love deep learning" → <code>[I] → love</code>, <code>[I, love] → deep</code>, <code>[I, love, deep] → learning</code>. Map tokens to ids, left-pad the inputs to the same length, and use the last token as the target class. The model is a classifier over the vocabulary: Embedding → LSTM → Linear(hidden, vocab_size), trained with <code>CrossEntropyLoss</code>.</p>' },
  { m: 'lstm', s: 'data',
    q: 'What does <code>pack_padded_sequence</code> do?',
    a: '<p>It tells the RNN the true length of each padded sequence so it skips the padding steps. Without it, a right-padded short sentence\'s final hidden state comes from running over padding. Pass the lengths (on CPU) and <code>enforce_sorted=False</code>, then <code>pad_packed_sequence</code> to unpack the output. Left-padding is a simpler alternative when you only use the last step.</p>' },
  { m: 'lstm', s: 'predict',
    q: 'How do you generate text with a trained next-word model?',
    a: '<p>Feed the seed text, take the logits for the last position, choose a word, append it, and repeat. <strong>Greedy</strong> decoding picks <code>argmax</code> — deterministic but repetitive. <strong>Sampling</strong> draws from <code>softmax(logits / T)</code>: temperature T &lt; 1 makes it safer, T &gt; 1 more varied. Top-k / top-p limit sampling to the most likely words.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 14 · Essentials ---------------- */
  { m: 'essentials', s: 'save', imp: true,
    q: 'Should you save the <code>state_dict</code> or the whole model?',
    a: '<p>Save the <code>state_dict</code> — an ordered dict of parameter and buffer tensors. To load it, build the model class and call <code>load_state_dict</code>. Pickling the whole model (<code>torch.save(model)</code>) ties the file to your exact class and folder structure, so it breaks when the code moves or changes.</p>',
    code: 'torch.save(model.state_dict(), "model.pt")\n\nmodel = MyNet()\nmodel.load_state_dict(torch.load("model.pt", map_location="cpu"))\nmodel.eval()' },
  { m: 'essentials', s: 'save',
    q: 'What goes into a training checkpoint?',
    a: '<p>Everything needed to resume exactly: the model\'s and the optimizer\'s state dicts (Adam keeps momentum buffers), the scheduler state, the epoch, the best metric so far and, if used, the <code>GradScaler</code> state.</p>',
    code: 'torch.save({\n    "epoch": epoch,\n    "model": model.state_dict(),\n    "optimizer": optimizer.state_dict(),\n    "scheduler": scheduler.state_dict(),\n    "best_acc": best_acc,\n}, "ckpt.pt")\n\nckpt = torch.load("ckpt.pt", map_location=device)\nmodel.load_state_dict(ckpt["model"])\noptimizer.load_state_dict(ckpt["optimizer"])\nstart_epoch = ckpt["epoch"] + 1' },
  { m: 'essentials', s: 'save',
    q: 'What do <code>map_location</code> and <code>weights_only</code> do in <code>torch.load</code>?',
    a: '<p><code>map_location</code> remaps stored tensors to another device — essential to load a GPU-saved file on a CPU-only machine. <code>weights_only=True</code> (the default since PyTorch 2.6) restricts unpickling to tensors and basic types, because loading an arbitrary pickle can execute code. Only set <code>weights_only=False</code> for files you trust.</p>' },
  { m: 'essentials', s: 'repro',
    q: 'How do you make PyTorch training reproducible?',
    a: '<p>Seed every random number generator, make DataLoader shuffling deterministic, and ask for deterministic kernels. Even then, results can differ across GPU models and library versions.</p>',
    code: 'import random, numpy as np\n\ndef seed_everything(seed=42):\n    random.seed(seed)\n    np.random.seed(seed)\n    torch.manual_seed(seed)            # CPU and all GPUs\n    torch.backends.cudnn.benchmark = False\n    torch.use_deterministic_algorithms(True)  # on CUDA also set CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG=:4096:8\n\ng = torch.Generator().manual_seed(42)\nloader = DataLoader(ds, batch_size=64, shuffle=True, generator=g)' },
  { m: 'essentials', s: 'amp',
    q: 'What is mixed-precision training?',
    a: '<p>Running most operations in float16 or bfloat16 halves memory use and speeds up tensor cores, while numerically sensitive operations stay in float32. <code>torch.autocast</code> picks the precision per operation. With float16, use <code>torch.amp.GradScaler</code> to scale the loss so small gradients don\'t underflow to zero; bfloat16 has float32\'s range and doesn\'t need scaling.</p>',
    code: 'scaler = torch.amp.GradScaler("cuda")\nfor xb, yb in loader:\n    optimizer.zero_grad()\n    with torch.autocast(device_type="cuda", dtype=torch.float16):\n        loss = loss_fn(model(xb), yb)\n    scaler.scale(loss).backward()\n    scaler.step(optimizer)\n    scaler.update()' },
  { m: 'essentials', s: 'compile',
    q: 'What does <code>torch.compile</code> do, and when does it help?',
    a: '<p><code>model = torch.compile(model)</code> traces your Python into graphs, fuses operations and generates optimised kernels. The first call is slow (compilation); later calls are faster — often 1.3–2× for training on modern GPUs. It helps most with large models and stable input shapes; changing shapes trigger recompiles (try <code>dynamic=True</code>). It needs no change to your training code.</p>' },
  { m: 'essentials', s: 'errors', imp: true,
    q: 'What are the most common PyTorch errors and their fixes?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>mat1 and mat2 shapes cannot be multiplied</strong> — wrong <code>in_features</code> in a Linear layer; print the shape before it.</li><li><strong>Expected all tensors to be on the same device</strong> — move the batch/labels to the model\'s device.</li><li><strong>expected scalar type Long but found Float</strong> — <code>CrossEntropyLoss</code> targets must be <code>.long()</code> class ids.</li><li><strong>Target size must be the same as input size</strong> — BCE needs targets shaped like the logits (<code>.float().unsqueeze(1)</code>).</li><li><strong>modified by an inplace operation</strong> — remove an in-place op on a tensor autograd still needs.</li><li><strong>CUDA out of memory</strong> — smaller batch, mixed precision, <code>no_grad</code> for evaluation, <code>loss.item()</code> instead of storing <code>loss</code>.</li></ul>' },
];
